🚽 Test case: issue #311 -- pointer arithmetic and indexing on struct
🚽 pointers. Both forms failed before:
🚽
🚽   p = p + 1;   Error: Cannot perform pointer arithmetic on a
🚽                type-erased pointer -- its pointee size is unknown
🚽   p[1].x       Error: Member access on a non-struct/union array element
🚽
🚽 The cost was that a helper could not walk a pool -- it could only be
🚽 handed one already-resolved `&pool[i]`, so the loops stayed inlined at
🚽 every call site.
🚽
🚽 ── Where the pointee size comes from ─────────────────────────────────
🚽 The issue proposed carrying a struct tag on the pointer's descriptor.
🚽 That turned out to be unnecessary: the tag is already on the Variable.
🚽 get_type_size_for_descriptor() takes only (VarType, pointer_level,
🚽 modifiers), so it genuinely cannot tell `gang Vec2 *` from
🚽 `gang Matrix *` -- both are VAR_STRUCT at pointer_level 1 -- and
🚽 correctly returns 0 rather than guessing. pointer_arith_scale() (ast.c)
🚽 does the second lookup that recovers the tag for exactly that case.
🚽
🚽 ── Why the sizes below are what they are ─────────────────────────────
🚽 Entity is {chad x; rizz id;} = 8 bytes, so `p + 1` advances 8 and
🚽 `p[2]` is 16 bytes in. The struct is deliberately NOT a single field:
🚽 a stride bug that used sizeof(chad) would still read element 0
🚽 correctly and only diverge from element 1 onward, which is what the
🚽 multi-element walks below are for.
🚽
🚽 ── Deliberately absent ───────────────────────────────────────────────
🚽 `(p + 1).x` -- member access directly on a parenthesized expression --
🚽 is still unsupported, and is a pre-existing general limitation of
🚽 resolve_struct_access() rather than anything specific to struct
🚽 pointers: it resolves identifier, member-access, array-access and
🚽 call bases, not arbitrary operations. Both forms this issue asked for
🚽 (`p = p + 1` then `p.x`, and `p[i].x`) work without it.
gang Entity {
    chad x;
    rizz id;
};

🚽 The motivating case: a helper that walks a pool it was handed as a
🚽 bare pointer, reading every element.
skibidi walk(gang Entity *p, rizz n) {
    flex (rizz i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
        yapping("  [%d] x=%.1f id=%d", i, p[i].x, p[i].id);
    }
}

🚽 ... and one that WRITES through the same pointer, so the element
🚽 addresses are proven to be lvalues, not just readable copies.
skibidi bump(gang Entity *p, rizz n) {
    flex (rizz i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
        p[i].x = p[i].x + 100.0;
    }
}

skibidi main {
    gang Entity pool[3];
    flex (rizz i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) {
        pool[i].x = i;
        pool[i].id = i * 10;
    }

    yapping("walk:");
    walk(&pool[0], 3);

    bump(&pool[0], 3);
    yapping("after bump:");
    walk(&pool[0], 3);

    🚽 the writes landed in the caller's own array, not a copy
    yapping("caller sees: %.1f %.1f %.1f",
            pool[0].x, pool[1].x, pool[2].x);

    🚽 arithmetic on the pointer itself, forwards and backwards
    gang Entity *p = &pool[0];
    yapping("p    x=%.1f", p.x);
    p = p + 2;
    yapping("p+2  x=%.1f", p.x);
    p = p - 1;
    yapping("p-1  x=%.1f", p.x);

    🚽 indexing relative to a pointer that is no longer at element 0
    yapping("p[1] x=%.1f", p[1].x);

    🚽 an ordinary scalar pointer is unaffected by any of this
    rizz nums[2];
    nums[0] = 4;
    nums[1] = 9;
    rizz *q = &nums[0];
    q = q + 1;
    yapping("scalar q=%d", *q);
    bussin 0;
}
